body:
## Summary

The `unnecessary_allof_wrapper` rule fires on the last entry of an `allOf` array when every entry follows the same `{ if, then }` conditional shape. Each entry is a sibling of the others — they conflict against each other even though no individual entry conflicts with the *parent* schema. The rule appears to consider only entry-vs-parent conflict, not entry-vs-sibling, so it flags the trailing entry as elevatable when it actually isn't.

## Environment

- `@sourcemeta/jsonschema` 15.5.0
- macOS (Darwin 25.4.0)
- Lint command: `jsonschema lint -r schemas/ schemas/`

## Minimal reproduction

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://example.com/example.schema.json",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "lifecycle": {
      "enum": ["active", "paused", "archived"]
    }
  },
  "allOf": [
    {
      "if": {
        "required": ["lifecycle"],
        "properties": { "lifecycle": { "const": "active" } }
      },
      "then": { "required": ["activeUntil"] }
    },
    {
      "if": {
        "required": ["lifecycle"],
        "properties": { "lifecycle": { "const": "paused" } }
      },
      "then": { "required": ["pauseReason"] }
    },
    {
      "if": {
        "required": ["lifecycle"],
        "properties": { "lifecycle": { "const": "archived" } }
      },
      "then": { "required": ["archivedAt"] }
    }
  ]
}
```

Lint output:

```
example.schema.json:N:M:
  Keywords inside `allOf` that do not conflict with the parent schema can be elevated (unnecessary_allof_wrapper)
    at location "/allOf/2/if"
example.schema.json:N:M:
    at location "/allOf/2/then"
```

The rule fires on `allOf[2]` (the last entry) only. The first two entries have `if/then` siblings inside `allOf` whose presence prevents the rule from firing on them. The trailing entry "looks elevatable" against the parent in isolation, but elevating it would create asymmetric structure: 2 gates inside `allOf` plus 1 standalone `if/then` at the parent.

## Why this looks like a false positive

The pattern here is intentional: N parallel conditional gates with uniform shape. Every entry is one of N sibling gates of the form `{ if: <lifecycle predicate>, then: <gate body> }`. The parent's lack of a top-level `if/then` is the structural intent — the symmetry is the point.

The rule's check that the trailing entry's keywords don't conflict with the parent is correct, but incomplete: it doesn't ask whether elevating *would still preserve the structural symmetry* with siblings.

## Possible directions

A few options that might be reasonable to consider — not prescriptive, just possible angles:

1. Skip the rule when `allOf` length ≥ 2 and every entry shares the same top-level keyword shape (e.g., all entries carry `if/then`).
2. Require the rule to verify the entry can be elevated without conflicting against any sibling, not just the parent.
3. Add a per-schema annotation (e.g., `"x-no-elevation": true`) the rule respects on a specific `allOf`.

Happy to iterate on a PR if you'd like to suggest a direction.

## Workaround

Globally disabling the rule via `-x unnecessary_allof_wrapper`. Documented locally with a per-rule rationale block in our lint wrapper.

Thanks for the great tool — `@sourcemeta/jsonschema` has been a clean fit for our pure-JSON-Schema project.
